  "mother": {
    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research serial output batching in src/permutations.cc while keeping enumeration logically unchanged. Compare replacing std::endl with '\\n' and disabling unnecessary stream synchronization against constructing output in a bounded byte buffer and issuing bulk writes. Avoid per-symbol formatted insertion and per-permutation flushing. Investigate a small range of buffer sizes, with a correct streaming path for rows larger than the buffer and a final drain. Preserve exactly '[ ' followed by each symbol and a space, then ']\\n'. Never retain the factorial-sized complete output. Use the unchanged automatic harness to establish which buffering strategy improves end-to-end time.",
        "B": "Research fused permutation generation and serialization. Maintain a preformatted row whose separators remain fixed; update only symbol positions affected by the next-permutation pivot, successor swap and suffix reversal, then copy the row into a bounded output buffer. Compare operating directly on the row's strided symbol positions against a compact symbol array with incremental row updates. This targets repeated formatting rather than reducing required output bytes. Preserve native char ordering, strict next-permutation comparisons and duplicate suppression. Handle empty input, one symbol and all-equal symbols safely. Keep buffering changes distinguishable from generator changes so G can measure their actual interaction.",
        "C": "Research whether ordered parallel generation can beat the best buffered serial approach on four ARM64 cores. Partition the actual input multiset into disjoint prefix subtrees arranged in lexicographic order, generating each subtree's suffix permutations without duplicates. Use overflow-safe, possibly saturated multinomial estimates to balance work and bypass parallelism for small workloads. Employ only facilities supported by the unchanged build. Bound output queues and give the earliest unfinished subtree progress guarantees; a single ordered writer must preserve exact output order without buffering entire subtrees or deadlocking behind later work. Compare low thread counts, including a serial fallback. Treat startup, synchronization and stdout serialization as likely obstacles, not assumed speedups.",
        "G": "Integrate only programs and evidence produced by A/B/C. First compare their validated standalone candidates, then measure a small set of compatible combinations, especially A's buffering with B's incremental row generation and the best serial kernel inside C. Do not assume gains add: copying, memory bandwidth, writer contention and synchronization can make integration slower. Preserve the fastest validated standalone option as a fallback. Use the existing correctness and timing procedures without modifying tests, oracle, compiler, target or budgets. Check semantic coverage of exact whitespace removal, native char ordering, duplicates, empty input and final buffer draining. Select the final complete program by the existing measured objective, treating small timing differences cautiously.",
        "analysis": "The relevant workload is Problem D, implemented solely in src/permutations.cc. Baseline correctness passed four cases; mean runtime was 0.152310 seconds, with samples spanning 0.150377–0.153777 seconds. No profiling or input sizes are available. The strongest source-grounded hypothesis is output overhead: every permutation performs multiple formatted insertions and std::endl forces a flush. Required output remains proportional to the number of distinct permutations times input length, so buffering should precede speculative parallelism. Preserve the source's exact semantics: remove only its six whitespace characters, sort using native char comparisons, emit distinct permutations lexicographically, and emit nothing for empty filtered input. The target has four ARM64 cores and no GPU; no optimization gain has yet been measured."
      },
